Nucl. Fusion 65 (2025) 086022 A.J. Barker et al Figure 3. Tritium production in 6Li at different layers throughout the blanket. The first instance of FLiBe for the best TBR performing configurations is shown with circular markers. In each instance, an increase in TBR is observed in the preceding Li layers. Also shown is a pure liquid Li blanket design for comparison (dashed line). though the best performing blanket designs all contain FLiBe, shown in figure 2. It is proposed that by including a propor- tion of molten salt in the rear of the blanket, FLiBe is act- ing as a reflector of neutrons and elevating tritium produc- tion in the preceding pure lithium layers, whilst also contrib- uting directly to the tritium production. The implication is that FLiBe increases tritium production in lithium through reflec- tion, rather than enhancing the overall (TBR) solely due to FLiBe being a more efficient breeder than pure liquid lithium. This is supported by figure 3, which shows the best performing iterations of the blanket, and displays tritium produced (from exclusively 6Li) in each layer. Referenced on the image is the first instance of FLiBe in each variation. Any lithium layers directly preceding (i.e. lower-layer number) the first appear- ance of FLiBe clearly show an increase in tritium production, while the highest performing blankets (shown in dark blue) all have their initial FLiBe layer in layer 4.5. The observed variations in tritium production between the hybrid scenarios are caused by a change in the ordering of Li and FLiBe layers from layer 5 onwards—see figure 2. The dashed line in figure 3 represents the TBR from a pure liquid lithium blanket and a constant decrease in tritium production is seen throughout the layers in contrast to the varied nature of the tritium production when FLiBe is included into the system. Here it is observed that FLiBe is outperformed at the rear of the blanket when compared to liquid lithium, though in the mixed segmentation scenarios, this under performance of FLiBe is counteracted by the increase in TBR in the initial layers of the blanket. This results in a larger overall TBR in the higher neutron flux areas of the blanket, rather than in the low TBR production areas at the rear of the blanket. For greater spatial resolution a fifty-layer blanket was cre- ated using the same methodology, as described above. The neutron flux per unit lethargy (neutron cm−2 per unit lethargy) as a function of neutron energy (group structure CCFE-709) and layer number, are shown in figures 4(a) and (b). These figures illustrate how the neutron flux spectrum is affected by penetration depth within the blanket, indicated by the layer number, with 50 representing the final layer, or the layer fur- thest from the plasma source. A comparison is made between a solely liquid lithium blanket, figure 4(a), and a hybrid dual breeder design, figure 4(b), where lithium is contained within the first 31 layers and layer 32–50 contain FLiBe. This was chosen because it most closely represents the best performing configuration in figure 2.

